  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nYesterday was Independence Day\u2014both locations were closed, which explains the zero revenue. This is structural, not operational. What matters is the underlying week and month. Port Washington is running 14.7% below DOW-adjusted expectation for July MTD ($11,870 actual vs. $13,917 expected)\u2014that's real performance loss, not calendar. Hicksville, by contrast, is running 8.6% *ahead* of expectation ($5,501 actual vs. $5,065 expected), a genuine bright spot. Year-to-date, Port Washington is up 50% and Hicksville is tracking strong on a new baseline; that momentum is worth protecting.\n\nThe capacity picture reveals the issue at Port Washington: you're running 4.5 groomer-days per day this month (up from 4.2 last month), yet revenue per groomer is only $526\u2014up modestly from $489 but insufficient to close the gap. The team is working more but not generating the expected lift. Hicksville tells the opposite story: fewer groomer-days (2.8 vs. 2.0 last month) but $428 per groomer-day, up from $391\u2014higher efficiency per available hand. Port Washington needs a hard look at upsell discipline and booking velocity; Hicksville's constraint is structural (not enough groomer hours scheduled).\n\nOne concern: Hicksville's visit-cadence drift is worsening\u2014the 12+ week gap bucket grew 2.4 percentage points. That's stealth churn. Port Washington's drift is improving (shrinking 2.2 pp), signaling retention recovery. The Hicksville lapse pool ($61K annual value) is actionable; prioritize reactivation calls this week.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Today you have 0 groomer(s) and 7 bather hours across both locations; tomorrow Port Washington returns with 5 groomer(s) and 7 bathers while Hicksville remains dark (0 groomer(s), 0 bathers); next 7 days show 39 total groomer-days across the business with only 43 bather hours, indicating a bather capacity constraint at Hicksville that is suppressing groomer throughput.**"
